/** @page code_flags Code Flags

    Builtin functions (and methods) can be tagged with flags that give some properties of the code to be executed as in the following sections.
    
    @section NOOP
    Code with this flag makes no calculations, but rather returns a constant value. This flag is given to function and method variants that return a default value depending on the type of argument(s). When variants with this flag are resolved at parse time, a @ref call-with-type-errors warning is raised (assuming this warning is enabled).

    @section RUNTIME_NOOP
    Code with this flag makes no calculations, but rather returns a constant value. This flag is given to function and method variants that return a default value depending on the type of argument(s). When variants with this flag are resolved at parse time, a @ref call-with-type-errors warning is raised (assuming this warning is enabled), unless Qore::PO_REQUIRE_TYPES or Qore::PO_STRICT_ARGS is set. If either Qore::PO_REQUIRE_TYPES or Qore::PO_STRICT_ARGS is set, then these variants are inaccessible; resolving to a variant with this flag set at parse time or run time causes an exception to be thrown. These variants are included for backwards-compatibility with qore prior to version 0.8.0 for functions that would ignore type errors in arguments.

    @section CONSTANT
    This legacy diagnostic flag indicates that discarding the return value is probably a mistake.
    It does not guarantee determinism or freedom from exceptions. Optimizations that depend on those
    properties require the explicit @ref PURE and @ref TOTAL contracts.

    @note This flag is not the same as a @ref const_methods "const method". Code flags describe side effects and
    exception behavior; const-method metadata describes whether an instance method can be called through a read-only
    receiver.

    @section RET_VALUE_ONLY
    This flag indicates that the function or method has no side effects but could throw an exception (see also @ref CONSTANT).

    @section NO_SIDE_EFFECTS
    The function or method does not mutate observable state.

    @section DETERMINISTIC
    The result depends only on the arguments and immutable state. The operation does not read clocks,
    random generators, process or thread IDs, the environment, or other mutable external state.

    @section NO_DOMAIN_THROW
    The operation raises no exception determined by its arguments or program state. Resource exhaustion,
    including allocation failure, can still raise an exception.

    @section PURE
    This combines @ref RET_VALUE_ONLY, @ref NO_SIDE_EFFECTS, and @ref DETERMINISTIC. Repeated calls with
    identical arguments can be combined, but a call can still raise a domain exception.

    @section TOTAL
    This combines @ref PURE and @ref NO_DOMAIN_THROW. In addition to combining identical calls, it permits
    eliminating unused calls and speculative evaluation, subject to resource-exhaustion exceptions.

    @section HOST_PORTABLE
    Results are bit-identical across conforming hosts. This stronger guarantee is required when a compiler
    evaluates a call on one host and stores its result in an executable for another host. It implies
    @ref DETERMINISTIC; determinism alone does not guarantee identical results on different hosts.

    @section DEPRECATED
    Code with this flag is deprecated and may be removed in a future version of %Qore; if a variant with this flag is resolved at parse time, a @ref deprecated warning is raised (assuming this warning is enabled).

    @section NAMED_ARGS
    Builtin functions and methods with this flag accept @ref function_named_arguments "named-argument calls".
    User-defined functions and methods accept named arguments automatically when their signature is resolved at parse
    time; builtin variants must opt in explicitly so parameter names are not exposed as a public calling interface by
    accident.

    @par Example:
    @code{.cpp}
binary ws_encode_frame(data message, int opcode, softbool masked,
        softbool fin = True, int rsv_bits = 0) [flags=RET_VALUE_ONLY,NAMED_ARGS] {
    // ...
}
    @endcode

    Callers can then use the qpp parameter names at the call site:

    @code{.py}
binary frame = ws_encode_frame(message: payload, opcode: WSOP_Text, masked: False, fin: True);
    @endcode

    @note Only fixed parameters are part of the named-argument surface. Varargs-only builtin variants should not use
    this flag, and public parameter names should be reviewed before enabling it.
*/
